## Proof

Let $(B_t)_{t>0}$ be a standard Brownian motion. We wish to compute $E(B_2 B_3 B_5)$.

**Key fact.** For any finite collection of times $0 < t_1 < \dots < t_n$, the vector $(B_{t_1}, \dots, B_{t_n})$ is jointly Gaussian with mean zero. This follows because each $B_{t_i}$ is a linear combination of the independent Gaussian increments $B_{t_1}, B_{t_2}-B_{t_1}, \dots, B_{t_n}-B_{t_{n-1}}$, each of which is centered and Gaussian.

In particular, $(B_2, B_3, B_5)$ is a zero-mean jointly Gaussian vector.

**Moment fact.** Every odd-order joint moment of a zero-mean multivariate normal distribution is zero. Indeed, if $X = (X_1, \dots, X_n) \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \Sigma)$, its joint moment generating function is
$$M(\theta) = E\,e^{\langle \theta, X\rangle} = \exp\!\big(\tfrac{1}{2}\theta^\top \Sigma\,\theta\big),$$
which is an even function of $\theta$ (depends only on $\theta$ through quadratic terms). Hence
$$E[X_{i_1}\cdots X_{i_k}] = \frac{\partial^k M}{\partial \theta_{i_1}\cdots\partial\theta_{i_k}}\Big|_{\theta=0}.$$
Since $M$ is even, all its odd-order partial derivatives at $\theta = 0$ vanish. Therefore every odd-order joint moment is $0$.

**Application.** $E(B_2 B_3 B_5)$ is a third-order (odd) joint moment of the zero-mean Gaussian vector $(B_2, B_3, B_5)$. By the fact above,

$$\boxed{E(B_2\, B_3\, B_5) = 0.}$$

